[{:db/id 17592186221434 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-34e7ccd4-a685-5844-a89f-5c57b735778f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186222044 :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of $f(t)={e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}$ for $s>2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}+2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+L\\{2t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t\\} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} & =\\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{{e}^{-2t}\\}+2L\\{t{e}^{2t}\\} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{(s-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{s+2}+\\frac{2}{(s-2)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221435 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b5d66a4d-16c1-5cfd-be68-7c518337e0a7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b5d66a4d-16c1-5cfd-be68-7c518337e0a7" :question/id #uuid "b5d66a4d-16c1-5cfd-be68-7c518337e0a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332062" :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-7t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0524c331-6564-52ef-aba2-9b3e8fe558a4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0524c331-6564-52ef-aba2-9b3e8fe558a4" :answer-field/id #uuid "0524c331-6564-52ef-aba2-9b3e8fe558a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b26e88d-f446-59c8-b01e-ca9d9e592906" "answer-7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" "answer-c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" "answer-ff77f603-3667-5d57-9f2f-4b85ac75f514" "answer-07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b26e88d-f446-59c8-b01e-ca9d9e592906" :answer/id #uuid "9b26e88d-f446-59c8-b01e-ca9d9e592906" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ horizontally." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" :answer/id #uuid "7dcba25b-c390-5edc-b740-7305051f595a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Scale the graph of $F(s)$ by a scale factor of $7$ vertically." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" :answer/id #uuid "c94dc5ec-cf48-51ff-9ce3-01c9fcc65d9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the left." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff77f603-3667-5d57-9f2f-4b85ac75f514" :answer/id #uuid "ff77f603-3667-5d57-9f2f-4b85ac75f514" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $7$ units to the right." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad" :answer/id #uuid "07208325-b6fe-56ff-ab78-18466949f3ad" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The Laplace transform is unchanged." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186402100 :question/problem "Consider a function $f(t)$ for which the Laplace transform is $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s).$ How can we obtain the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-9t}f(t)\\}$ from the graph of $F(s)?$" :question/worked-solution "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nThis implies that multiplying a function $f(t)$ by ${e}^{at}$ shifts the corresponding Laplace transform horizontally along the $s$-axis.\n\n- If $a>0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the right.\n\n- If $a<0,$ the graph of $F(s)$ shifts to the left.\n\nIn our case, to get the graph of $L\\{{e}^{-9t}f(t)\\},$ we must shift the graph of $F(s)$ by $9$ units to the left." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-34e7ccd4-a685-5844-a89f-5c57b735778f" :question/id #uuid "34e7ccd4-a685-5844-a89f-5c57b735778f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332187" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}, &  & s>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nuse linearity of Laplace transforms and the first shifting theorem to find the Laplace transform of  $f(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}$ for $s>5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the linearity properties of Laplace transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}+7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =L\\{2{e}^{-3t}\\}+L\\{7{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that, for $s>0,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{n}\\} & =\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}\\} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first shifting theorem states that if $L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$ then $L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} & =\\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{f(t)\\} & =2L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\}+7L\\{{t}^{2}{e}^{5t}\\} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+3}+7\\cdot \\frac{2}{(s-5)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis transform is valid for $s>5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cbbbb7ac-d5cd-51d2-b97c-84f0232078de"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cbbbb7ac-d5cd-51d2-b97c-84f0232078de" :answer-field/id #uuid "cbbbb7ac-d5cd-51d2-b97c-84f0232078de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eb1cbeb1-e8a7-518d-a6fb-cbc0586e7fc5" "answer-a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" "answer-5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" "answer-f81a2466-a55c-5f7e-b7c2-eccdac0d7ed6" "answer-83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0e38764"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb1cbeb1-e8a7-518d-a6fb-cbc0586e7fc5" :answer/id #uuid "eb1cbeb1-e8a7-518d-a6fb-cbc0586e7fc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" :answer/id #uuid "a6286f17-855d-5d36-bc99-ac311ecdbde2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" :answer/id #uuid "5812230e-36ca-591f-b89d-48a8b7e1dcc2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s+3}+\\frac{7}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f81a2466-a55c-5f7e-b7c2-eccdac0d7ed6" :answer/id #uuid "f81a2466-a55c-5f7e-b7c2-eccdac0d7ed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s+5)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0e38764" :answer/id #uuid "83050161-c9bb-5cd0-b5d2-7a04f0e38764" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{s-3}+\\frac{14}{(s-5)^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186304946 :question/problem "Consider a function $d(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{d(t)\\}=D(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Starting from the definition of the Laplace transform, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-9t}d(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\cdot {e}^{-9t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that $D(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t,$ where $s>{s}_{0}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-(s+9)t}\\,d(t)\\,\\text{d}t=D(s+9),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\ns+9>{s}_{0}\\,⇒\\,s>{s}_{0}-9.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
